An `XmlNamedValue` is a value together with the name of the element it is written under. Use it for a member that has a choice of elements, set with several `[XmlElement<T>]` attributes, when two of the elements hold the same type. The type of the value can't say which element it belongs to, so the value carries the element's name instead.

```csharp
public sealed class ContactCard
{
    [XmlElement<string>("Home")]
    [XmlElement<string>("Work")]
    public List<XmlNamedValue> Phones { get; set; } = [];
}
```

```csharp
var options = new XmlSerializerOptions { OmitXmlDeclaration = true };
var card = new ContactCard
{
    Phones = { new XmlNamedValue("Work", "555-0100"), new XmlNamedValue("Home", "555-0199") },
};

string xml = XmlSerializer.Serialize(card, options);
output.WriteLine(xml);

ContactCard read = XmlSerializer.Deserialize<ContactCard>(xml, options)!;
foreach (XmlNamedValue phone in read.Phones)
{
    output.WriteLine($"{phone.Name}: {phone.Value}");
}
```

The code produces the following output:

```text
<ContactCard><Work>555-0100</Work><Home>555-0199</Home></ContactCard>
Work: 555-0100
Home: 555-0199
```

Declare the member as `XmlNamedValue` for one value, or as a collection of it for a sequence. `Name` must be the name of one of the member's elements, and `Value` must be of the type that element holds; anything else throws a `NotSupportedException` when it is written. A `Value` that is `null` is written as an element marked `xsi:nil="true"`. Set `Namespace` only when two of the member's elements have one local name in two namespaces. A value that was read has the namespace its element was declared with.

See [When two elements hold the same type](/guides/polymorphism#when-two-elements-hold-the-same-type) and the [polymorphism sample](https://github.com/CloudNimble/XmlEssentials/blob/main/src/CloudNimble.XmlEssentials.Samples/16_Polymorphism.cs).
